<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · Sacred · c. 1916 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Jerusalem (And did those feet in ancient time)</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi>And did those feet in ancient time</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A poem by William Blake, set to music by Hubert Parry in 1916, that became one of England's best-loved songs.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>William Blake</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Hubert Parry (orchestration by Parry 1918, and by Edward Elgar 1922)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>English; sung in churches, schools, political and civic gatherings in England</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Felpham, Sussex, England (the poem); London (the music)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>poem in the preface to Milton, title page dated 1804, printed c. 1808; music written 10 March 1916</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by William Blake (c. 1808); music by Hubert Parry (1916; he died in 1918))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The words are a short poem by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-blake">William Blake</a> in the preface to his epic Milton: A Poem in Two Books. The title page bears the date 1804, probably when Blake began the plates, but the poem was printed around 1808. Blake wrote much of Milton while living in a cottage at Felpham in Sussex; local tradition at Lavant, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichester">Chichester</a>, holds instead that he wrote this poem in a public house there. The poem draws on an old story, repeated in Milton's History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, that Joseph of Arimathea came to preach to the ancient Britons; the idea that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> himself visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, often assumed to lie behind it, is according to the folklorist A. W. Smith not attested as an oral tradition before the early 20th century. The poem asks questions rather than asserting such a visit, and its last part is usually read as a call to build a just society in England. Its mills have been read as the factories of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>, perhaps the steam-powered Albion Flour Mills near Blake's home, burnt in 1791; as the established Church; or as part of Blake's own mythology. Blake supported the French Revolution and in 1803 was tried at Chichester for sedition over words spoken in conversation, and acquitted.</p><p>The poem was little known for a century. In 1916, as casualties of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> mounted, the Poet Laureate Robert Bridges included it in his anthology The Spirit of Man and asked Hubert Parry to set it for a meeting of the Fight for Right campaign at the Queen's Hall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, as simple music an audience could join in. Parry, uneasy about the campaign's patriotism, agreed for the sake of Bridges and his former student Walford Davies, who was to conduct; he wrote the tune on 10 March 1916 and the first performance followed on 28 March. In May 1917 Parry withdrew his support from Fight for Right.</p><p>The song was saved for wider use by the women's suffrage movement. The National Union of Women's Suffrage Societies had taken it up in 1917, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millicent-garrett-fawcett">Millicent Fawcett</a> asked to use it at a Suffrage Demonstration Concert on 13 March 1918; Parry orchestrated it for the occasion and agreed that it should become the Women Voters' Hymn, assigning the copyright to the union. When the union was wound up in 1928, the copyright passed to the Women's Institutes, which still close meetings with it; it entered the public domain in 1968. The title &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>&quot; appears to date from about 1918, though Parry always used the poem's first line. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-elgar">Edward Elgar</a> re-scored it for very large orchestra for the Leeds Festival in 1922, and his version is the one usually heard at the Last Night of the Proms.</p><p>The song has been claimed across the political spectrum. Labour used it in the 1945 general election, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-attlee">Clement Attlee</a> promised to build a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and it is sung at the close of Labour Party conferences as well as at Conservative and Liberal Democrat gatherings. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> is said to have preferred it to &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot;. Some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> clergy hold that it is not strictly a hymn, since it is not a prayer to God, yet it is sung in many churches, on Saint George's Day, at the 2011 royal wedding in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> and in schools across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and beyond. It opened the 2012 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Olympics, topped a 2019 poll of the United Kingdom's favourite hymns, and is used as an English anthem by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> Cricket Board and, at times, by rugby league and the Commonwealth Games team; England has no official anthem. In 2020 a new arrangement by Errollyn Wallen, sung at the Last Night of the Proms, caused much controversy.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The poem asks whether a holy presence once walked in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and whether heaven was briefly built there, set against the grim industry of the poet's own day. It ends in a vow of spiritual struggle: not to rest until a just and holy city is built in England. Its mood moves from wonder to resolve.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And did those feet in ancient time</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Walk upon England's mountains green?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And was the holy Lamb of God</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On England's pleasant pastures seen?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And did the Countenance Divine</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Shine forth upon our clouded hills?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And was Jerusalem builded here</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Among those dark Satanic mills?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Bring me my bow of burning gold:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Bring me my arrows of desire:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Bring me my spear: O clouds unfold!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Bring me my chariot of fire.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I will not cease from mental fight,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Nor shall my sword sleep in my hand</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Till we have built Jerusalem</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In England's green and pleasant land.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: William Blake, Milton (1804). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Jerusalem_(Blake)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="vyKIjbCKkCE" data-title="Jerusalem (And did those feet in ancient time): The Choir of King's College, Cambridge, Parry's setting"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">The Choir of King's College, Cambridge, Parry's setting</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel The Choir of King’s College, Cambridge - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vyKIjbCKkCE" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Jerusalem, Parry's orchestration for the Suffrage Demonstration Concert</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1918</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Jerusalem, Edward Elgar's orchestration for the Leeds Festival</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1922</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Brain Salad Surgery (Emerson, Lake &amp; Palmer)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1973</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording on the Moog Apollo synthesiser, banned as a single</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Jubilee, film (Derek Jarman)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1977</span><span class="sg-chain-h">punk version used in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Chariots of Fire, film</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1981</span><span class="sg-chain-h">title taken from the poem; the hymn sung in its final scenes</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Fall</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1988</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Amsterdam EP (Simple Minds)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1989</span><span class="sg-chain-h">instrumental version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Chemical Wedding (Bruce Dickinson)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1998</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the full poem set in a new song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Isles of Wonder, opening ceremony of the 2012 Summer Olympics</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2012</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in, and inspired segments of the ceremony</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Errollyn Wallen's new arrangement for the Last Night of the Proms</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2020</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new setting of the poem</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Love Is Noise (The Verve)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">reworks lines of the poem</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Neo Yokio, television series</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody as the anthem of a fictional city state</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The legend of Joseph of Arimathea's mission to Britain, as told in Milton's History of Britain</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the poem's story</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">2 Kings 2:11 (Elijah's chariot of fire)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted image</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Book of Revelation and the Song of Moses (Deuteronomy 32)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">themes and imagery</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Numbers 11:29</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quotation printed beneath the poem</span></li></ul></section>
